<statement>
<p>
A native wolf species has been reintroduced into a national forest.  Originally 300 wolves were transplanted, and after 3 years the population had grown to 658 wolves.  If the population grows exponentially according to the formula <m>\displaystyle{P}_{{t}}={P}_{{{0}}}{\left({1}+{r}\right)}^{{t}}</m>.</p>
<p>
(a) Find the growth rate.  Round your answer to the nearest tenth of a percent.</p>
<p>
r = <fillin characters="6"/><percent/></p>
<p>
(b) If this trend continues, how many wolves will there be in ten years?</p>
<p>
<fillin characters="6"/> wolves</p>
<p>
(c) If this trend continues, how long will it take for the population to grow to 1000 wolves?  Round your answer to the nearest tenth of a year.</p>
<p>
<fillin characters="6"/> years</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><ul><li>29.9</li><li>4113</li><li>4.6</li></ul></p>
</answer>
<solution><p>Based on the information given</p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{658}={300}{\left({1}+{r}\right)}^{{3}}</m>.</p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle\frac{{658}}{{300}}={\left({1}+{r}\right)}^{{3}}</m>.</p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{\left(\frac{{658}}{{300}}\right)}^{{\frac{{1}}{{3}}}}={1}+{r}</m>.</p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{\left(\frac{{658}}{{300}}\right)}^{{\frac{{1}}{{3}}}}-{1}={r}</m>.</p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{r}={29.927638887994}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{r}\approx{29.9}</m></p>
<p>The amount after 10 years is </p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{P}{\left({10}\right)}={300}{\left({1.2992763888799}\right)}^{{{10}}}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{P}{\left({10}\right)}={4112.791731136}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{P}{\left({10}\right)}\approx{4113}</m></p>
<p>To find how long until the population reaches 1000 wolves, we solve the following.</p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{1000}={300}{\left({1.2992763888799}\right)}^{{t}}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle\frac{{1000}}{{300}}={1.2992763888799}^{{t}}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{\ln{{\left(\frac{{1000}}{{300}}\right)}}}={\ln{{\left({1.2992763888799}^{{t}}\right)}}}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{\ln{{\left(\frac{{1000}}{{300}}\right)}}}={t}{\ln{{\left({1.2992763888799}\right)}}}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle\frac{{\ln{{\left(\frac{{1000}}{{300}}\right)}}}}{{\ln{{\left({1.2992763888799}\right)}}}}={t}</m></p>
<p>
  <m>\displaystyle{4.6}\approx{t}</m>.</p>
</solution>
</myopenmath>